\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces For a 3 player 1-D game there can be 5 different equilibrium types. In the sub-figures there are two plots: on the left, the player positions in 3-D representation and on the right, the final player positions in 1-D. In \cref  {fig6:a} the players converge to a unique equilibrium at large $\sigma $ ($\sim 0.25$). For \cref  {fig6:b} the players converge to a dispersed non-unique equilibrium (determined by initial positions) ($\sigma \sim 0.17$). \cref  {fig6:c} is slightly different, the players stay near the equilibrium in \cref  {fig6:b} but they now have dampening cycles. \cref  {fig6:d} demonstrates how the players could possibly be stuck in a cycle. Finally, in \cref  {fig6:e} the players reach an equilibrium around one of the peaks of the resource distribution. Each of these figures assumes that the players are not starting in the same positions, since having the same position will lead to players converging to the coalesced equilibrium in \cref  {fig6:a}.}}{15}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig6}{{2}{15}{For a 3 player 1-D game there can be 5 different equilibrium types. In the sub-figures there are two plots: on the left, the player positions in 3-D representation and on the right, the final player positions in 1-D. In \cref {fig6:a} the players converge to a unique equilibrium at large $\sigma $ ($\sim 0.25$). For \cref {fig6:b} the players converge to a dispersed non-unique equilibrium (determined by initial positions) ($\sigma \sim 0.17$). \cref {fig6:c} is slightly different, the players stay near the equilibrium in \cref {fig6:b} but they now have dampening cycles. \cref {fig6:d} demonstrates how the players could possibly be stuck in a cycle. Finally, in \cref {fig6:e} the players reach an equilibrium around one of the peaks of the resource distribution. Each of these figures assumes that the players are not starting in the same positions, since having the same position will lead to players converging to the coalesced equilibrium in \cref {fig6:a}}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig6@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][14][]15}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The main figure, is a bifurcation diagram demonstrating how the resource distribution mode split $\alpha $ impacts the $\sigma $ values for bifurcations to occur in. Here we see that as $\alpha $ increases the symmetric Nash equilibrium becomes unstable at higher $\sigma $ values, leading to the emergence of the non-unique equilibria at higher $\sigma $ values. In each subfigure, a,b,c,d we take a random sample of 10,000 initial conditions and use gradient ascent to find the equilibria they converge to. Each color represents a different equilibrium type: blue is the symmetric nash stable region, purple is the $(1,1,1)$ region, yellow is an estimated cycling region, and red is the $(2,1)$ equilibrium. Using a fixed initial condition we can estimate the boundary for the where cycles occur and where the $(2,1),(1,2)$ equilibria dominate.}}{16}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig313_1}{{3}{16}{The main figure, is a bifurcation diagram demonstrating how the resource distribution mode split $\alpha $ impacts the $\sigma $ values for bifurcations to occur in. Here we see that as $\alpha $ increases the symmetric Nash equilibrium becomes unstable at higher $\sigma $ values, leading to the emergence of the non-unique equilibria at higher $\sigma $ values. In each subfigure, a,b,c,d we take a random sample of 10,000 initial conditions and use gradient ascent to find the equilibria they converge to. Each color represents a different equilibrium type: blue is the symmetric nash stable region, purple is the $(1,1,1)$ region, yellow is an estimated cycling region, and red is the $(2,1)$ equilibrium. Using a fixed initial condition we can estimate the boundary for the where cycles occur and where the $(2,1),(1,2)$ equilibria dominate}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig313_1@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][14][]16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Here we see the different equilibria that can occur for $\sigma =0.16$, for fixed $\alpha =0.5$. This occurs in the yellow region (region b) in \cref  {fig313_1}. There are two main types of equilibria: the symmetric nash equilibrium and the asymmetrical non-unique equilibria, the stable $(1,1,1)$ equilibria and the unstable $(2,1)$ equilibria.}}{16}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig313_2}{{4}{16}{Here we see the different equilibria that can occur for $\sigma =0.16$, for fixed $\alpha =0.5$. This occurs in the yellow region (region b) in \cref {fig313_1}. There are two main types of equilibria: the symmetric nash equilibrium and the asymmetrical non-unique equilibria, the stable $(1,1,1)$ equilibria and the unstable $(2,1)$ equilibria}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig313_2@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][14][]16}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces By fixing the initial positions we can then generate a bifurcation diagram demonstrating how the resource distribution mode split $\alpha $ impacts the $\sigma $ values for each equilibrium type. Here we see that as $\alpha $ increases the symmetric Nash equilibrium becomes unstable at higher $\sigma $ values, leading to the emergence of the non-unique equilibria at higher $\sigma $ values, as do the bounds of the regions of their stability.}}{17}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig313_3}{{5}{17}{By fixing the initial positions we can then generate a bifurcation diagram demonstrating how the resource distribution mode split $\alpha $ impacts the $\sigma $ values for each equilibrium type. Here we see that as $\alpha $ increases the symmetric Nash equilibrium becomes unstable at higher $\sigma $ values, leading to the emergence of the non-unique equilibria at higher $\sigma $ values, as do the bounds of the regions of their stability}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig313_3@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][17][]17}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.1.4}Four Player Dynamics}{17}{subsubsection.3.1.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces For a 4 player system some similar behavior occurs. If we restrict the players initial conditions to be in the hyper plane where $(x,y,y,z)$, then we see the equilibrium the above heatmap for 4 players. There are still 2 main types of equilibria: the symmetric nash equilibrium and the asymmetrical non-unique equilibria. The asymmetric equilibria include the stable $(2,2)$ equilibria, the unstable $(3,1)$ and $(1,2,1)$ equilibria.}}{18}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig313_4}{{6}{18}{For a 4 player system some similar behavior occurs. If we restrict the players initial conditions to be in the hyper plane where $(x,y,y,z)$, then we see the equilibrium the above heatmap for 4 players. There are still 2 main types of equilibria: the symmetric nash equilibrium and the asymmetrical non-unique equilibria. The asymmetric equilibria include the stable $(2,2)$ equilibria, the unstable $(3,1)$ and $(1,2,1)$ equilibria}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\newlabel{fig313_4@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][17][]18}{}{}{}}
